      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_90bf582a",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "islamophobia_stereotype",
      "label": "Islamophobia Stereotype",
      "description": "The meme relies on the harmful stereotype that Muslim women, particularly those wearing hijabs, are associated with violence or terrorism. This is a common and dangerous form of Islamophobia that equates religious attire with danger, reinforcing prejudice and fear. The text 'nuns don't randomly explode' is a crude metaphor that weaponizes this stereotype by implying that Muslim women are inherently explosive or dangerous, while suggesting nuns are not, despite both wearing head coverings.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_d40c8178",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "hijab",
      "label": "Hijab",
      "description": "The hijab is a headscarf worn by Muslim women as a religious and cultural practice, symbolizing modesty and faith. The meme misrepresents this as a dangerous or explosive object, which is a distortion of its actual meaning and purpose. This misrepresentation is central to the meme's offensive message.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_5c9f22a5",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "nun",
      "label": "Nun",
      "description": "A nun is a female member of a religious order, typically in a Christian denomination, who has taken vows of poverty, chastity, and obedience. The meme uses the nun as a contrast to the Muslim woman, implying that religious head coverings are only offensive or dangerous when worn by Muslims, not by Christians. This is a form of religious bias and misrepresentation.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1c6b3711",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "terrorism_association",
      "label": "Terrorism Association",
      "description": "The meme implicitly references the real-world association between Islam and terrorism, which is a common and harmful stereotype. This association is used to justify the offensive comparison and to imply that Muslim women are inherently dangerous or prone to violence, which is a false and dangerous generalization.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_017234f9",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "religious_head_covering",
      "label": "Religious Head Covering",
      "description": "The meme juxtaposes two types of religious head coverings — the nun's habit and the hijab — to highlight a perceived difference in how they are viewed. This is based on the assumption that one is 'acceptable' (nun's habit) and the other is 'offensive' (hijab), which is a form of religious bias and misrepresentation.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c1cd125d",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "harmful_joke_emotion",
      "label": "harmful joke emotion",
      "description": "The emotion expressed is one of malicious humor or offensive jest, rooted in prejudice and designed to provoke fear and hostility. The meme uses a crude, violent metaphor to demean and stereotype Muslim women, implying they are dangerous or prone to violence, while contrasting them with nuns who are not similarly judged. This emotion is not genuine amusement but rather a calculated attempt to spread misinformation and reinforce negative stereotypes.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_92c4a0ec",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_id_1",
      "label": "The text 'nuns don't randomly explode' is mapped onto the visual entity of the Muslim woman wearing a hijab.",
      "description": "The meme uses the text 'nuns don't randomly explode' to create an analogical mapping where the Muslim woman in the hijab is implicitly equated with an explosive device. This mapping is part of a harmful stereotype that weaponizes the hijab by associating it with violence or terrorism, while contrasting it with the nun's habit, which is not similarly judged. The text explicitly refers to the second visual entity (the Muslim woman) as if she were an explosive, creating a direct and offensive analogical link between the textual metaphor and the visual representation.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fde656b3",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the textual material implying a judgmental stance",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the textual material 'nuns don't randomly explode' which functions as a deictic statement that implicitly positions the viewer as someone who judges or finds the second image (Muslim woman in hijab) offensive, while not judging the first (nun). This projection is not explicit with 'you' or 'me' but is implied through the rhetorical question and the contrastive framing, making the user feel they are the one making the judgment call.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c1836d58"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_e7442fbc",
